1
00:00:00,079 --> 00:00:03,539
Record numbers help people find
and distinguish documents during

2
00:00:03,539 --> 00:00:05,530
a handover or a data migration.

3
00:00:06,010 --> 00:00:09,750
This walkthrough starts on Records
Settings in the fictional Firehouse

4
00:00:09,750 --> 00:00:11,300
fifty-one training department.

5
00:00:11,680 --> 00:00:16,690
We will inspect the numbering controls,
save one change, verify what persisted

6
00:00:16,870 --> 00:00:18,520
and restore the original pattern.

7
00:00:19,030 --> 00:00:22,239
The department name and settings
page should match the department

8
00:00:22,239 --> 00:00:23,569
you intend to administer.

9
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:25,309
Scroll to Record numbering.

10
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:29,940
Number pattern currently contains
the prefix token, a hyphen, the

11
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:33,789
four-digit year token, another
hyphen and the sequence token.

12
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:36,959
The help below explains
what each token writes.

13
00:00:37,329 --> 00:00:41,839
Prefix uses the record type prefix,
year includes the numbering year,

14
00:00:42,130 --> 00:00:43,880
and sequence supplies the counter.

15
00:00:44,329 --> 00:00:48,319
Changing this pattern does not renumber
records that already have numbers.

16
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:51,239
Read that impact before
applying a new format.

17
00:00:51,440 --> 00:00:54,039
Sequence digits is four
in this department.

18
00:00:54,469 --> 00:00:57,880
The help shows that four digits
writes a zero-padded value

19
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:00,099
such as zero one eight four.

20
00:01:00,519 --> 00:01:03,200
This control accepts three
through eight digits.

21
00:01:03,610 --> 00:01:08,079
Changing the width keeps each sequence's
next number; it is not the same as

22
00:01:08,079 --> 00:01:09,860
choosing a different next counter.

23
00:01:10,269 --> 00:01:13,419
Definitions that have their own
numbering keep their own width.

24
00:01:13,829 --> 00:01:17,190
In this exercise, we leave the
four-digit setting as it is.

25
00:01:17,389 --> 00:01:19,899
Numbering year starts has a month and day.

26
00:01:20,340 --> 00:01:22,340
January first is selected here.

27
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:25,960
The displayed help explains
how a fiscal-year start affects

28
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:27,689
sequences that include the year.

29
00:01:28,099 --> 00:01:30,939
This setting is separate from
call numbering, which has its

30
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,830
own year start on Call Settings.

31
00:01:33,189 --> 00:01:37,099
Review the policy your department
actually follows before changing either.

32
00:01:37,499 --> 00:01:40,890
This demonstration keeps the
existing January-first value.

33
00:01:41,190 --> 00:01:46,790
Record type prefixes lists each type,
its default and a Number prefix box.

34
00:01:47,170 --> 00:01:52,390
The visible defaults include
Run, R U N; Training, T R N;

35
00:01:52,790 --> 00:01:56,659
Work, W R K; and Meeting, M T G.

36
00:01:57,099 --> 00:01:59,249
Leave a box blank to keep its default.

37
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:04,150
The help explains that a new prefix
starts its own sequence, and types given

38
00:02:04,150 --> 00:02:06,349
the same prefix share one sequence.

39
00:02:06,779 --> 00:02:10,630
Prefixes take effect when the number
pattern includes the prefix token.

40
00:02:11,040 --> 00:02:14,390
Choose them deliberately rather
than using a cosmetic change that

41
00:02:14,480 --> 00:02:16,410
accidentally joins two counters.

42
00:02:16,630 --> 00:02:20,190
For this local exercise, replace
the two hyphens in Number

43
00:02:20,190 --> 00:02:21,750
pattern with underscores.

44
00:02:22,159 --> 00:02:25,730
Keep the same prefix, four-digit
year and sequence tokens.

45
00:02:26,099 --> 00:02:29,099
The example beside the field
now uses underscores too.

46
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:31,940
This is still an unsaved form value.

47
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:36,060
The format of the example is useful
feedback, but it does not prove

48
00:02:36,060 --> 00:02:39,279
the department has saved the change
or issued a new record number.

49
00:02:39,479 --> 00:02:43,659
Scroll to the bottom of Records Settings,
where Save and Cancel are visible.

50
00:02:44,170 --> 00:02:46,999
Review your intended edit
before choosing Save.

51
00:02:47,380 --> 00:02:51,290
This demonstration changes the number
pattern only; it does not submit a

52
00:02:51,290 --> 00:02:55,389
record, raise a counter or request
delivery to an external service.

53
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:59,700
Use Save once, then verify the
returned settings page instead

54
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:03,009
of assuming that typing in the
field completed the configuration.

55
00:03:03,250 --> 00:03:06,569
The returned page displays
Records settings saved.

56
00:03:06,959 --> 00:03:09,500
This confirms that the
settings form was accepted.

57
00:03:09,939 --> 00:03:13,670
We still need to verify the particular
value we intended to change.

58
00:03:14,090 --> 00:03:17,399
A general success notice is
useful, but reading the saved

59
00:03:17,399 --> 00:03:21,130
field and its effect gives an
administrator a more complete check.

60
00:03:21,270 --> 00:03:24,830
Reopening Records Settings shows
the underscore-separated pattern

61
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:27,930
in Number pattern, with the
matching example alongside it.

62
00:03:28,410 --> 00:03:30,779
This is a fresh page load after saving.

63
00:03:31,290 --> 00:03:34,679
Compare the full pattern,
including each token and separator,

64
00:03:34,739 --> 00:03:36,040
with your intended format.

65
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:40,950
The result confirms persistence of this
local setting; the example is still an

66
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:43,519
example rather than a newly issued record.

67
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:47,199
The saved pattern changes Next
numbers for twenty twenty-six.

68
00:03:47,690 --> 00:03:52,839
The Run and Training previews now have
underscores and end in zero zero zero one.

69
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:56,209
The text around the sequence
token changed, so this

70
00:03:56,209 --> 00:03:58,079
format has its own sequence.

71
00:03:58,499 --> 00:04:01,880
Raise next to is a separate
control for carrying on from

72
00:04:01,880 --> 00:04:03,440
previously issued numbers.

73
00:04:03,850 --> 00:04:06,720
Leave it alone when you do
not intend to raise a counter.

74
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:10,200
Viewing this table does not
issue the previewed numbers.

75
00:04:10,450 --> 00:04:14,559
After the exercise, the original
hyphen-separated pattern was restored.

76
00:04:15,030 --> 00:04:19,570
Record numbering now shows the original
prefix, year and sequence arrangement.

77
00:04:20,089 --> 00:04:23,979
Restore a practice change only when
that is your intended configuration.

78
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:27,920
Changing separators back is a
format decision; it is not a

79
00:04:27,920 --> 00:04:31,449
way to erase previously issued
numbers or reset a counter.

80
00:04:31,670 --> 00:04:35,599
The original format now shows
Training ending in zero zero zero

81
00:04:35,630 --> 00:04:39,549
three, the same next-number preview
it had before the demonstration.

82
00:04:40,030 --> 00:04:42,599
Run ends in zero zero zero one.

83
00:04:43,139 --> 00:04:46,829
Restoring the earlier format
resumed its existing sequence rather

84
00:04:46,829 --> 00:04:48,489
than restarting Training at one.

85
00:04:48,899 --> 00:04:51,479
No new records were issued
during this exercise.

86
00:04:51,989 --> 00:04:55,960
When importing from another system,
review Raise next to against the

87
00:04:55,960 --> 00:04:59,559
numbers already issued before
applying a higher starting point.

88
00:04:59,679 --> 00:05:04,080
Other record numbers has separate rows
for Records requests, Occupancies,

89
00:05:04,210 --> 00:05:07,250
Inspections, Permits and Investigations.

90
00:05:07,600 --> 00:05:12,469
Each visible row has a Number pattern,
Sequence digits and a next-number area.

91
00:05:12,879 --> 00:05:16,159
The help explains that blank
patterns keep the built-in numbers

92
00:05:16,399 --> 00:05:19,700
and that these types follow the
record-numbering year start above.

93
00:05:20,130 --> 00:05:22,880
Review the particular row
you intend to administer.

94
00:05:23,280 --> 00:05:26,540
This recording inspects those
controls without saving a custom

95
00:05:26,540 --> 00:05:28,489
pattern for these other record types.

96
00:05:28,669 --> 00:05:32,730
A consistent numbering policy helps
security teams find post reports,

97
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:36,770
wildland contractors distinguish
crew-support work, and industrial

98
00:05:36,770 --> 00:05:38,820
teams coordinate equipment records.

99
00:05:39,200 --> 00:05:43,060
Community paramedicine and mental
health teams can organize appropriate

100
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:46,719
service documentation, while
search-and-rescue teams can distinguish

101
00:05:46,719 --> 00:05:48,330
training and support records.

102
00:05:48,719 --> 00:05:52,090
Fire and E M S departments can
use the same pattern review.

103
00:05:52,539 --> 00:05:56,859
Agree on the format, inspect the
correct controls, save deliberately

104
00:05:56,889 --> 00:06:00,129
and verify both persistence
and the next-number preview.


